>I deplore violence and find myself in an increasingly dangerous
>society.  Looking around the world and then at America I see a direct
>correlation  between increased violence and the availability of
>handguns.

Correlation is not causation.  The jurisdictions of the U.S. that have the
most stringent handgun laws tend to have the highest rates of violent
crime... for example, New York City.

I think it would be wonderful if no one ever had to use a gun.  I also
think it would be wonderful if everyone could transmute elements and
levitate.  Unfortunately, we live in the real world, where handgun laws
only keep guns out of the hands of the law-abiding, leaving them at the
mercy of criminals.  To paraphrase Drexler, Peterson, and Pergamit in
_Unbounding the Future_ (speaking of prohibition of nanotech research), the
question to ask is not "Would we be better off without X?" but "Would
efforts to ban X make the situation any better?"

It has been demonstrated to my satisfaction that prohibition, by law, is
effectively impossible for _anything_ small enough for one person to carry.
 Please reread the previous sentence until you understand it, because it's
crucial to what follows.  (If you understand it but don't believe it, go
study the available statistics on the prevalence of illegal drugs,
handguns, whatever... then come back.)

Now, given that _some_ people in society are going to have guns no matter
what you do, whom do you want to have them?  If there is a law against
guns, criminals are going to have them and law-abiding people are not (by
definition).  Do you prefer that distribution, or would you prefer an
arrangement that gives the non-criminal a chance of survival?

>People who used to bloody their noses now shoot each other
>and often take a few innocents along with them.

"Used to" when?  Guns have been in existence for centuries, usually
unregulated except by the ability to afford one.  Prior to that, it was
swords, knives, clubs, rocks, and muscle.  In the absence of firearms, a
5'2", ninety pound woman is completely at the mercy of a 6'4", 215 pound
man.  If both wear guns, they are roughly equals, ceteris paribus.  

>Try to imagine
>yourself as the parent of a 4 year old killed from a stray round in a
>drive-by; I doubt you would be so tickled that guns can be had by all. 

Being of a more rational frame of mind, I would probably be outraged that
guns were available only to law-breakers and not to decent citizens like
myself.  You try to imagine yourself as the brother of a woman who
successfully defended herself from rape and probable murder by a gang of
thugs in a shopping mall parking lot, because she was armed.  I doubt you
would be so ungrateful then for the existence of handguns.

The principle of spontaneous order, as practiced by Extropians, says, "I
behave in ways that incentivize the people around me to behave in ways
consistent with the kind of society I want."  What would happen to
burglary, for example, if handgun ownership were much more prevalent? 
Potential burglars, knowing the odds are good that they may surprise an
armed householder, will seek a less dangerous line of work.  Note that my
possession of a gun helps not only me but my unarmed neighbors as well: 
the burglar may know that one in ten homes (I wish) have firearms, but he
can't tell which apartments have the guns and which ones don't.  Similarly,
if a significant fraction of women went armed, then rapists would be
incentivized to think twice before bothering any woman.

>I see no evolutionary, enlightened or progressive purpose served and
>wonder what Extropian goals are advanced by such preventable tragedies.

None, obviously; but gun prohibition will not prevent such tragedies.  If
anything, gun prohibition is a contributing factor in their prevalence.  

>Because I hold these views and am curious about how other views respond
>to them am told this list is probably not for me.  I am not interested
>in debating, or agreeing with most people on the list.  I am interested
>in aquiring information and hearing other points of view.  Do I belong
>on this list?  
>-Rev. Bud Struggle

If your views are immune to argument, I would say the list is better off
without you, yet.  If, on the other hand, you're willing to consider the
evidence, and perhaps change your mind, or if you're willing to provide the
rest of us with the crucial information that will cause us to change _our_
minds, then perhaps your presence serves a purpose.  (Note:  If you do
decide to change our minds, you will need hard data and very persuasive
arguments -- emotional appeals to the prejudices of the masses won't work,
because we're a bunch of statistical outliers who think for ourselves.)
