Nope, nope, nope... this is the crux of the dispute, and neurologically,
you're in error.  You say "our nervous system ... groups [sensations] into
perceptions" and claim that this happens before "consciousness processes
them".  However, the entire action of the nervous system is influenced by
consciousness.  Experiments have shown that it is possible to condition a
cat not to hear the sound of a buzzer.  Not "not _react_ to the sound", but
in fact not to _hear_ it.  (The researchers were monitoring the auditory
bundle in the brain electronically.)  Perceptual agencies of the nervous
system (to use Minsky's terminology) shape their interpretation based on
consciousness.  In humans, similar experiments have been done with the
perception of pain from needles under hypnosis... the signal from the
stimulus arises in the hand where the needle is poked, but the signal does
not reach the brain.

The nervous system is one big neural net; there isn't a boundary to be
drawn saying "this part is mechanical; this part is conscious."  It's _all_
mechanical, and it's _all_ (to varying degrees) conscious.

